 { Gilberto Milos – Marc Narciso Dublan, Mexico City 2010 Learning objective: This game illustrates how White can carry out the standard minority attack in the Carlsbad, and how to attack the weaknesses once they are created. } 1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. cxd5 exd5 5. Bg5 c6 6. e3 Be7 7. Bd3 Nbd7 8. h3 O-O 9. Qc2 Re8 10. Nf3 Nf8 11. Bf4 { I would like to paraphrase something my former coach Bartlomiej Macieja said to me about a year before I wrote this book. He said, 'People usually talk about good and bad bishops, but it should be quite clear that White’s bad bishop is rather powerful on f4 or g5.' That is, White’s ‘bad’ bishop is actually an excellent piece as long as it is outside the pawn chain, unlike the previous game. Hence it makes sense for Black to trade it with: } Bd6 ( { PH: Actually it was better to first gain a tempo with } 11... Ng6 $1 12. Bh2 { and only now offer the exchange with } Bd6 13. Bxd6 Qxd6 { . Now if White castles short, the move ...Ng6 was an important extra tempo. For example } 14. O-O Qe7 15. Rab1 Ne4 $1 16. b4 { and here } Qf6 $5 { already creates theats such as ...Bxh3, and if } 17. Nxe4 dxe4 18. Bxe4 Rxe4 $1 19. Qxe4 Bf5 { White's queen is trapped. } ) 
12. Bxd6 Qxd6 13. O-O { This is a standard set-up for this pawn structure; in fact there are over 100 games in my database with this exact position. } Be6 ( { Another option was: } 13... Qe7 14. Rab1 Ne4 15. b4 a6 16. a4 ( { If } 15... Be6 15. Bxe4 dxe4 17. Nd2 Bf5 { chances are level. Black may continue with ...Ng6-h4 aiming for a kingside attack. } ) 
Bf5 17. b5 Nxc3 18. Qxc3 Bxd3 19. Qxd3 cxb5 20. axb5 a5 { Black has a playable position, though the d5-pawn is a small weakness. } ) 
14. Rab1 N6d7 ( { Probably better was: } 14... a5 15. a3 N8d7 16. Rfc1 ( { Black is ready to meet } 15... N6d7 15. b4 $6 { with } b5 $1 { when he achieves reasonable counterplay associated with ...Nb6-c4. White is unable to take advantage of the weakness of the c6-pawn: } 17. Rfc1 Nb6 18. Qb3 axb4 19. Qxb4 Qxb4 20. axb4 Nc4 { With a level game. } ) 
h6 17. Na4 { White is getting ready to play b2-b4. } Ne4 $1 { The right moment for this reaction. } 18. Nd2 ( { It is not possible to grab the pawn with } 17... N6d7 17. Bxe4 dxe4 19. Qxe4 $2 { due to } Bb3 { winning the knight. } ) 
Bf5 19. Bxe4 Rxe4 $1 20. Nxe4 Bxe4 21. Qd1 Qg6 22. Qf1 { Black has a comfortable position, and agreed to a draw in the game Krivoshey – Klovans, Germany 1997. } ) 
15. b4 Nb6 16. Nd2 Rad8 $6 { Black plays without a clear plan, and misses his chance to play the standard ...a7-a6 defensive move. } ( { It was not too late for Black to play: } 16... a6 17. a4 g6 ( { But not } 17... Nc4 $6 18. Bxc4 dxc4 19. b5 $1 { ⩲ as the c4-pawn is permanently weakened. } ) 
18. Rfc1 $5 { ⩲ The b4-b5 break is coming sooner or later, but at least Black will only have to deal with one weakness – probably the c6-pawn – instead of two weaknesses as in the game. } ( { It is premature to play } 17... Rad8 17. b5 $6 axb5 19. axb5 c5 $1 20. dxc5 Qxc5 { when Black has enough counterplay to compensate for the isolated queen pawn, because of the open a- and c-files, as well as the c4-square for one of Black’s knights. } ) 
 ) 
17. Ne2 $1 { White wants to prevent Black from responding ...c6-c5 against the break b4-b5. } ( { An interesting alternative was } 17. b5 c5 18. dxc5 Qxc5 19. Nf3 Rc8 20. Rbc1 $14 { where White enjoys a slightly favourable version of the isolani. As we studied in the game Vitiugov – Bologan from Chapter 1, the absence of Black’s dark-squared bishop deprives him of useful counterplay. Compare this to the note after Black’s 16th move, and see how in this case the a-file is now closed, limiting Black’s options. } ) 
Bc8 ( { Now it is too late for } 17... a6 { due to: } 18. Nb3 $1 Qe7 ( { The tempting } 18... Nc4 $2 { fails after } 19. Nc5 $1 Qe7 ( 19... Na3 $2 { is no help: } 20. Qb2 Nxb1 21. Nxb7 Qc7 22. Nxd8 { and wins. } ) 
20. Bxc4 dxc4 21. Nxe6 Qxe6 22. Nf4 Qe4 23. Qxc4 $16 { winning a pawn. } ) 
19. Nc5 Bc8 20. a4 $16 { leaving Black unprepared to meet b4-b5. } ) 
18. b5 { White achieves the b4-b5 break under optimal conditions, creating at least one serious weakness in Black’s queenside. } ( { PH: There's also nothing wrong with the slower } 18. a4 $5 ) 
cxb5 19. Rxb5 { The d5-pawn is now very weak. } Bd7 20. Ra5 Nc8 ( { A better defence was } 20... Ra8 21. Qc5 Qxc5 22. dxc5 Nc8 23. Rb1 Bc6 24. Nd4 Ne7 $14 { and Black seems to hold for a while. } ) 
 ( { But not } 20... a6 $2 21. Rb1 { followed by Qb3, when Black’s queenside is about to collapse. } ) 
21. Nf4 Bc6 22. Bb5 $1 { Clarifying the position on the queenside; the positional threat is Bxc6 forcing the creation of a very weak pawn on c6. } Ne6 ( { It is no better to play: } 22... Bxb5 23. Rxb5 Nb6 24. a4 { threatening a4-a5 winning the d5-pawn. } a6 ( { Or } 24... Rc8 25. Qb3 $16 ) 
25. Rb3 $16 { Now the b7-pawn becomes a serious target. } ) 
23. Bxc6 bxc6 24. Nd3 $16 { This position deserves some comment. Since Black decided not to play ...a7-a6 earlier in the game, both the a2- and a7-pawns remain present. This plays a crucial role, as Black needs to worry about protecting both the a7- and c6-pawns. } Rd7 25. Rc1 Rc7 26. Qc3 Nb6 27. Nc5 { To be followed by Na6-b4 in order to pressure the c6-pawn. } Qd8 ( { Of course not } 27... Nxc5 $4 28. dxc5 { . } ) 
28. Na6 Rc8 29. Qa3 Re7 30. Nb4 { Note how both the a7-pawn and the c6-pawn are under attack; Black’s defence has collapsed. } Qd6 $6 ( { A better defence was } 30... Rec7 31. Rxa7 c5 32. Rxc7 Rxc7 33. dxc5 Nxc5 $16 { though White has an extra pawn for no compensation. } ) 
31. Rxc6 $1 { A nice tactical blow in a strategically winning position. } Rxc6 32. Nxc6 Qxa3 ( { Not } 32... Qxc6 $4 33. Qxe7 { and White wins at once. } ) 
33. Rxa3 Rc7 34. Nb4 { White is a pawn up, while the d5- and a7-pawns remain serious weaknesses. } ( { There is nothing wrong with } 34. Nxa7 { , though White needs to calculate the consequences of } Rc1 35. Kh2 Rc2 { . } ) 
Kf8 35. Kf1 Ke8 36. Ra5 Nc4 ( { Passive defence with } 36... Rd7 { does not enable the position to be held, for example: } 37. Ke2 g6 ( 37... Kd8 $6 38. Rxa7 { winning a second pawn. } ) 
38. Kd3 Nd8 39. Nb1 { followed by Nc3 winning easily. } ) 
37. Nxc4 dxc4 { The passed pawn on c4 is simply another weakness in Black’s camp. } 38. Ke2 Rb7 39. a3 Nc7 40. Kd2 Nb5 41. Kc2 Kd7 ( 41... Nxa3 { fails to help after } 42. Rxa3 Rxb4 43. Rxa7 { as the c4-pawn is practically lost, say after Ra2 and Kc3. } ) 
42. Ra6 h5 43. Nd5 h4 44. Nc3 Nd6 ( { Or } 44... Nxc3 45. Kxc3 Rc7 46. e4 { is winning. } ) 
45. e4 { White has arranged his pieces in harmony and suppressed all counterplay; the rest is very simple. } f6 46. f3 Nc8 47. Na4 Ke8 48. Rc6 Ne7 49. Rxc4 Ng6 50. Nc5 Rc7 51. Kb3 Nf4 52. Rc2 a5 53. Kc4 g5 54. d5 Rc8 55. Kd4 Rb8 56. e5 fxe5+ 57. Kxe5 Rb1 58. Ne6 Nd3+ 59. Kf6 Kd7 60. Nxg5 Rb3 61. Nf7 Ne1 62. Re2 Nxg2 63. Ne5+ { Final remarks 1. Black could have faced fewer problems if he had played ...a7-a6 at the right time. Then after a future b4-b5, Black would have had some chances to protect the weak c6-pawn. 2. The sequence of moves before White’s tactical blow 31. Rxc6 would not have worked so easily for White if Black did not have a weak a7-pawn. Essentially this was not a tactical mistake, but the consequence of a prior positional mistake (not playing 14... a5, or not playing 15... a6 or 16... a6). } 
 1-0

