 { Alexander Onischuk – Leinier Dominguez Perez, FIDE World Cup, Tromsø 2013 Learning objective: This game illustrates how Black may neutralize White’s minority attack with a precise sequence of trades and manoeuvres. } 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 Bb4+ 4. Bd2 Bxd2+ 5. Qxd2 d5 6. Nc3 O-O 7. e3 Qe7 8. cxd5 exd5 9. Bd3 Nbd7 10. O-O c6 11. Qc2 Re8 12. Rab1 Ne4 13. b4 Ndf6 { The first new move of the game. } ( { Previously } 13... g6 { had been played, after which } 14. b5 Nxc3 15. Qxc3 c5 16. dxc5 Nxc5 17. Nd4 Bd7 18. Rfc1 { gave White only a microscopic advantage in Khanukov – Kholmov, Arco 2001. } ) 
14. Ne5 ( { The immediate } 14. b5 $5 { is met by } c5 $1 15. dxc5 Nxc5 16. Nd4 Bd7 17. Rfd1 Rac8 { when the weak b5-pawn and the outpost on e4 give Black good compensation for the isolani. } ) 
 ( { PH: The computer prefers the surprising } 14. Nd2 { , which abandons the kingside, but it appears Black doesn't really have an attack anyway. } Bf5 15. Ndxe4 Nxe4 16. Nxe4 Bxe4 { and after } 17. b5 $14 { White keeps a very small advantage. } ) 
Nxc3 15. Qxc3 Ne4 16. Qc2 ( { The alternative } 16. Bxe4 dxe4 17. b5 { does not give White anything after } c5 $11 { since the c5-pawn cannot be captured: } 18. dxc5 $4 Qxe5 $19 ) 
g6 $1 { An important move, with the idea of ...Bf5 reinforcing Black’s control of the e4-square. } ( { Instead } 16... Nf6 $6 { would allow White to weaken Black’s queenside with } 17. Rfc1 ( { But not the premature } 16... g6 16. b5 $2 c5 $11 { again, since White’s knight on e5 needs protection. } ) 
a6 18. a4 $14 { followed by the unstoppable b4-b5. } ) 
17. Rfc1 ( { White cannot take the pawn with } 17. Bxe4 dxe4 18. Qxe4 $2 { because } f6 { wins a piece. PH: However, } 18. Rfc1 $1 { intends b5 with a small advantage for White, combining the minority attack with a centralized knight on e5. } ) 
Bf5 18. Rb3 ( { Again it is impossible to play } 18. b5 $2 { because } c5 $1 { ⩱ followed by ...c5-c4 enables Black to take the lead. } ) 
 ( { The alternative } 18. Nf3 { with the idea of b4-b5 is refuted tactically by: } a5 $1 19. b5 ( { Or } 19. bxa5 Rxa5 $15 ) 
cxb5 { White’s queen on c2 experiences problems, for example: } 20. Bxb5 ( { Even worse is: } 20. Rxb5 $2 Rac8 21. Qb2 Nxf2 $3 22. Bxf5 Nd3 $1 { The key move. } 23. Bxd3 Qxe3 24. Kh1 Rxc1 $19 ) 
Rec8 21. Qb3 Nc3 $1 22. Ra1 ( 22. Rxc3 $2 Rxc3 23. Qxc3 Bxb1 $19 ) 
Qb4 $1 { And Black suddenly takes the lead on the queenside. } ) 
a6 19. a4 Nd6 20. Bxf5 Nxf5 21. h3 { Now a little bit of context: this game took place during the FIDE World Cup (a knockout event) where players confront each other twice to decide who advances to the next round. Having lost the first encounter, Onischuk absolutely needed a win in order to stay in the match, but to my surprise (I was following the game online) after playing 21. h3 he offered a draw, thus being eliminated! I thought about this for quite some time and came to agree with his decision. } ( { The truth is that Black has arranged his pieces so well that once again } 21. b5 $2 { fails due to } axb5 22. axb5 c5 $1 { ∓ since the c5-pawn cannot be captured by } 23. Qxc5 $2 { because of } Qxc5 { when Black wins after either } 24. dxc5 Rxe5 ( { Or } 24. Rxc5 $2 Ra1 ) 
 ) 
Nd6 $1 { Preventing b4-b5, White has nothing useful to do, meanwhile Black can speculate with potential kingside threats if he wishes, or he may just wait. As an example I offer the following variation: } 22. Nd3 { Hoping to gain something with the slow manoeuvre Nb2-d1-c3 followed by b4-b5. } Kg7 23. Nb2 Kg8 { Black is simply waiting for White to do something more concrete. } 24. Nd1 $6 { Allowing Black a nice reaction: } f5 $1 { A well-timed decision. } 25. Nb2 { Aiming towards Nd3-e5 now that the e5-square lacks protection, but Black has a powerful reply. } ( { If White instead proceeds with his b4-b5 idea with } 25. Nc3 { he meets the strong } f4 $1 26. exf4 Qf6 27. Rd1 Qxf4 $15 { where Black’s position is preferable due to the vulnerability of the d4-pawn. } ) 
 ( { If } 25. g3 { Black may just accept the challenge with } g5 $5 { ⩱ or choose the alternative: } h5 $5 26. h4 g5 $15 ) 
f4 $1 26. exf4 Qf6 27. Rd1 Nf5 28. Rbd3 Re4 $17 { White is in trouble. Final remarks 1. What is important to take from this example is that White’s minority attack does not always work. If Black knows exactly how to react, he can perfectly well prevent the b4-b5 break. 2. In particular, playing ...a7-a6 is necessary and the idea ...Ne4-d6 covering the b5-square, should not be forgotten. } 
