 { Radoslaw Wojtaszek – Ildar Khairullin, Aix-les-Bains 2011 Learning objective: This game explains how White may proceed with a queenside attack if Black’s queenside pawns are arranged as a7-b6-c7 instead of the typical a7-b7-c6. } 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. Nf3 b6 5. e3 Bb7 6. Bd3 O-O 7. O-O d5 8. cxd5 exd5 9. a3 Bd6 10. b4 { Everything is being played according to modern theory; in fact there are over 250 games with this position. One should pay close attention to Black’s pawn structure on the queenside. The most typical Carlsbad structure has pawns on a6-b7-c6-d5, which makes the b4-b5 break quite powerful. In this case, it might seem that White has little to hope for since b4-b5 does not create any weak pawns, but this is not quite the case. White’s plan is to first gain some space on the queenside with b4-b5 and a3-a4, weakening the c6-square and 'isolating” the d5-pawn, and then either play Ba3xd6 in order to weaken the c7-pawn, or blow up the centre with e3-e4. In both cases, Black’s counterplay is associated with placing a knight on e4 and then creating a kingside attack, possibly through ...f7-f5 and ...g6-g5, as illustrated in the note to Black’s 10th move. } a6 ( { An alternative was: } 10... a5 11. b5 Nbd7 12. Bd2 $6 ( { Instead I would have recommended } 11... a6 11. Qb3 { followed by a3-a4 and Ba3 trading Black’s dark-squared bishop to weaken the c7-pawn. } ) 
Ne4 { A good decision; it is crucial for Black to gain control of the e4-square, not only to prevent an eventual e3-e4 break, but also to gain attacking prospects on the kingside. } 13. a4 Qe7 14. h3 f5 { Reinforcing the knight on e4 and increasing the attacking chances against White’s monarch. } 15. Qc2 Ndf6 16. Rac1 Rae8 17. Ne2 g5 { Black obtained a dangerous attack in Malaniuk – Annageldyev, Alushta 2007. } ) 
11. Qb3 ( { PH: The other attacking plan for White in such positions, which is quite popular nowadays, is to play } 11. Ne5 Nbd7 12. f4 { - of course this leads to a very different type of position than what we see in this game. } ) 
Qe7 12. Rb1 Nbd7 13. a4 Rfb8 $6 { The rook is rather useless on this square. } ( { Preferable was: } 13... Rfe8 14. b5 axb5 ( { Worse is } 14... a5 15. Ra1 Rad8 16. Ba3 { as trading dark-squared bishops weakens the c7-pawn: } h6 17. Bxd6 cxd6 ( { Or } 17... Qxd6 18. Rac1 $14 ) 
18. Rac1 $14 { when White has a pleasant position, as in Ionov – Shaposhnikov, St Petersburg 2003. } ) 
15. axb5 Ne4 $1 { When White must probably accept the challenge and sacrifice an exchange with: } 16. Nxd5 Bxd5 17. Qxd5 Nc3 18. Qb3 Nxb1 19. Qxb1 ( { Or } 18... Rfb8 18. Bxb1 Nf6 20. e4 Nxe4 21. Re1 Qe6 22. Qxe6 Rxe6 23. Bxe4 Ra1 $13 { when Black will regain the two bishops for a rook. } ) 
Nf6 { Controlling the e4-square, with an approximately level game. } ) 
 ( { PH: I think the best move is } 13... c6 $5 { , intending to meet } 14. b5 { with } axb5 15. axb5 c5 { and } 14. a5 { with } b5 { . The structure looks a bit ugly for Black, but it works quite well - Black's control over c4 is just as important as White's control over c5. } ) 
14. b5 axb5 15. axb5 g6 ( { The typical } 15... Ne4 $6 { does not work well due to } 16. Nxd5 Bxd5 17. Qxd5 Nc3 18. Qc4 Nxb1 19. Bxb1 $14 { with very good compensation for the exchange, as Black cannot prevent the advance e3-e4 with: } Nf6 $6 20. e4 $1 Nxe4 $2 21. Re1 $18 { This variation illustrates why Black’s rook on e8 was important. } ) 
16. h3 h6 ( { Again, the desired } 16... Ne4 { is met by } 17. Nxd5 Bxd5 18. Qxd5 Nc3 19. Qc6 Nxb1 20. Bxb1 $14 { followed by e3-e4. } ) 
17. Re1 { Beginning to prepare the break e3-e4. } Ra7 ( { Once again: } 17... Ne4 18. Nxd5 $1 { ⩲ } ) 
18. Bb2 ( { An interesting alternative was } 18. Bxg6 $5 fxg6 19. e4 { with a dangerous initiative, for example } Nh5 20. e5 Ba3 21. Bxh6 Nf8 ( 21. Bb2 Qe6 $2 22. Ng5 $1 { +- followed by Nxd5. } ) 
22. g4 Ng7 23. Nxd5 { with an attack. } ) 
Qe6 { Protecting d5, hoping for the desired 19...Ne4. } 19. Nd2 { Reinforcing the control of e4, White is almost ready to break in the centre. } Rba8 { Note how ineffective Black’s play is on the queenside, as after so many moves there is still no semblance of a threat. } ( { Unfortunately for Black } 19... Ne4 $2 { simply loses a pawn after } 20. Ncxe4 dxe4 21. Qxe6 fxe6 22. Nxe4 { . } ) 
20. Qc2 Bf8 21. e4 $1 { The desired break at the right time. White gains a very powerful initiative in the centre, and Black is unable to deal with it. } dxe4 ( { It was preferable to simply give up a pawn with } 21... Qe7 22. exd5 ( { Or } 21... dxe4 21. e5 $5 { ± } ) 
Qd8 23. Bc4 $16 { though Black still needs to be careful with tactical shots, such as d5-d6 threatening Qxg6. } ) 
22. Ndxe4 Bxe4 ( 22... Nxe4 { is no improvement: } 23. d5 $1 Qe7 ( { Or } 23... Bxd5 24. Nxd5 Qxd5 25. Bxe4 Qxb5 { when White does not bother with Bxa8, but instead } 26. Bxg6 $1 { +- } ) 
24. Nxe4 { (threatening Nf6) } Qd8 25. Qc3 f6 26. Bc2 { followed by Qg3 with a decisive attack. } ) 
23. Nxe4 Bg7 ( { The alternative } 23... Nxe4 { is a nice illustration of White’s positional dominance; after } 24. Bxe4 Re8 25. Re3 $1 { White’s advantage does not rely on a specific tactical shot, but rather on his great piece coordination, and Black’s inability to protect the back rank, as illustrated by the variation: } Qd6 26. Rbe1 Bg7 27. Qc4 Rf8 28. Bc2 Nf6 ( 28... Raa8 29. Re6 $1 { +- } ) 
29. Bb3 Raa8 30. Re7 $18 ) 
24. Bc4 Qf5 25. g4 $1 { An unlikely yet highly effective resource, taking advantage of the awkward location of Black’s queen. } ( { The more standard } 25. Re3 $5 { should also be winning. } ) 
Qf3 ( { Instead } 25... Qf4 $2 { loses to } 26. Bc1 Qf3 27. Rb3 { and the queen is trapped. } ) 
 ( { There is nothing to hope for after } 25... Nxg4 26. hxg4 Qxg4 27. Ng3 { . } ) 
26. Re3 Qf4 27. Ng3 { Threatening Qxg6. } Nf8 28. Bxf7+ $1 { A nice blow! The attack is overwhelming and White finished off the game with ease. } Kh7 ( { The bishop is immune, because of } 28... Kxf7 $2 29. Qb3 Ne6 30. Rxe6 Kf8 31. Rbe1 { followed by Re7, and White wins. } ) 
29. Bb3 Rd8 30. Rbe1 Rd7 31. Re5 Ra8 32. Qc4 Rad8 33. R1e4 $1 Qd2 ( { Not } 33... Nxe4 $4 34. Qg8 { . } ) 
34. Bc3 Qc1+ 35. Kg2 Nd5 36. Ne2 Qa3 37. Nf4 Bxe5 38. Nxd5 Rxd5 39. Rxe5 c6 ( { Again, not } 39... Rxe5 $4 40. Qg8 { . } ) 
40. Bb4 Qa8 41. bxc6 b5 42. Qxd5 Rxd5 43. Bxd5 ( { Black resigns. The final position is quite picturesque. The strong c6-pawn, together with White’s bishops, makes the position impossible to defend, for example: } 43. Bxd5 Qd8 44. c7 Qxc7 45. Re7 { Final remarks 1. Whenever Black arranges his pawns as in the game, with a7-b6-c7, White must carefully analyze whether he will be able to dispute Black’s control of the e4-square. If that is not the case, something is likely to go wrong. 2. If White can dispute the e4-square, then the plan b4-b5 followed by an eventual e3-e4 is very powerful, as it opens many lines for attack. Positionally, it also makes sense, as Black’s backward c7-pawn and the c6-square may become vulnerable after e3-e4, especially if light-squared bishops are traded on e4. 3. Finally, Black had nothing to hope for down the a-file or on the queenside for that matter. Hence Black’s 13th move proved to be an utter waste of time. } ) 

 1-0

