[White "5) The Carlsbad Formation"]
[Black "Magnus Carlsen vs. Viswanathan Anand"]
[Result "1-0"]

 { Magnus Carlsen – Viswanathan Anand, Tal Memorial, Moscow 2013 Learning objective: This game shows how White can successfully carry out the central break e3-e4. } 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. e3 O-O 5. Ne2 d5 6. a3 Be7 7. cxd5 ( 7. cxd5 exd5 { is analyzed in the next game. } ) 
Nxd5 8. Bd2 Nd7 9. g3 b6 10. Nxd5 exd5 11. Bg2 Bb7 { The game Getz – Agdestein clearly illustrated how White’s bishop is often poorly placed on d2 in this structure. Did Carlsen miss this concept? Of course not! } 12. Bb4 $1 { Finding an unusual route to make use of this bishop. } Nf6 ( { If } 12... c5 13. dxc5 bxc5 14. Bc3 { White has a slightly favourable version of the hanging pawns structure, as he has already traded one minor piece and his bishop on g2 puts pressure on the d5-pawn. } ) 
 ( { Dubious is } 12... Bxb4 $6 13. axb4 Qe7 14. Qb3 Nf6 15. O-O { ⩲ followed by Rfc1 when White can exert pressure down the a- and c-files. } ) 
13. O-O Re8 14. Rc1 c6 15. Bxe7 Rxe7 { The dark-squared bishops are off the board and White has a small advantage since the c6-pawn is already a weakness due to the advance ...b7-b6. } 16. Re1 { The reader should pay close attention to this move, as it is the beginning of a new plan in our toolbox. } ( { The plan } 16. b4 $6 { is not as powerful since Black will respond to an eventual b4-b5 with ...cxb5 as the d5-pawn is well protected. } ) 
 ( { PH: Perhaps the most clever move is the computer's } 16. Rc3 $1 { , preparing Qc2 and keeping Black busy with the defense of the c6-pawn and the c-file in general. } ) 
Qd6 17. Nf4 Bc8 ( { Transposing into a hanging pawns structure with } 17... c5 18. dxc5 bxc5 { puts Black under definite pressure after: } 19. Qc2 Rc8 20. Red1 $14 ) 
18. Qa4 Rc7 ( { PH: The logical move would be } 18... Bd7 { , but then } 19. Qb4 $1 { is very annoying. The ending after } Qxb4 20. axb4 { is quite unpleasant for Black - c6 and d5 are weak and White can even consider doubling rooks on the a-file. } ) 
19. f3 $1 { Excellent; Carlsen chooses the perfect occasion to execute the typical plan e3-e4. } Be6 ( { The try } 19... c5 { is met by } 20. e4 $1 { posing great problems since after } dxe4 $2 ( { or } 20... Be6 21. dxc5 Rxc5 22. Rcd1 $16 ) 
21. fxe4 $18 { the threat e4-e5 is even stronger, as there is a discovered attack on the a8-rook as well. } ) 
20. e4 dxe4 $2 { Underestimating White’s tactical resources. } ( { A better defence was } 20... Qd7 21. Nxe6 ( { Or } 20... dxe4 20. e5 c5 22. Qxd7 Nxd7 23. Ne2 $14 ) 
Qxe6 22. e5 Ne8 23. Rc3 $14 { when White enjoys a pleasant advantage in a flexible position. } ) 
21. fxe4 { Note how the influence of White’s bishop on g2 has been enhanced by the plan f2-f3 and e3-e4. Black’s position is uncomfortable due to multiple threats, such as e4-e5 winning a piece. } Qd7 ( { If } 21... b5 22. Qb4 $1 Qxb4 23. axb4 { Black is in severe trouble. For example, } Rac8 24. Rc3 Bg4 25. e5 Ne8 26. Rec1 { and Black’s position falls apart. } ) 
22. d5 $1 { A nice tactical blow, breaking Black’s defence. } cxd5 23. Qxd7 Rxd7 24. Nxe6 fxe6 25. Bh3 $1 { The key move; Black cannot avoid losing a pawn. } Kh8 ( { Another option was: } 25... Re8 26. exd5 Rdd8 $1 ( { Inaccurate is } 26... Kh8 $6 27. Rxe6 $1 ) 
27. Bxe6 Kh8 28. Red1 $16 { White has a full extra pawn, but Black survives a little longer. } ) 
 ( { Worse is } 25... Re7 $6 26. exd5 { . } ) 
26. e5 Ng8 27. Bxe6 Rdd8 $6 { Making the win easier. } ( { Instead } 27... Re7 28. Bxd5 Rd8 { allows Black to resist longer: } 29. Bb3 $5 { ± } ) 
28. Rc7 d4 29. Bd7 $1 { Black resigns in view of Rd1 followed by Rxd4. Final remarks 1. White’s move 12. Bb4 should be remembered. Carlsen understood the dark-squared bishop was useless inside the pawn chain as we learned earlier, so he found an unusual way to trade it. 2. The e3-e4 break is particularly strong with a bishop on g2. Most importantly, this break is even stronger if Black has played ...b7-b6 weakening the c6-pawn. 3. Black’s capture 20...dxe4 is illogical, as it clears the way for the bishop on g2. After this mistake Black’s position became extremely fragile, causing an early collapse. } 
 1-0

