[Black "Yuriy Kuzubov vs. Zahar Efimenko"]
[Result "0-1"]

 { Yuriy Kuzubov – Zahar Efimenko, Kiev 2012 Learning objectives: 1. This is an example of how Black may neutralize White’s attempt to break with e3-e4. 2. After the break e3-e4 is carried out under unfavourable circumstances, the game turns into a bad version of a white isolani, which Black wins convincingly. } 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. e3 O-O 5. Ne2 d5 6. a3 Be7 7. cxd5 exd5 8. g3 Nbd7 9. Bg2 Nb6 10. Qc2 a5 { When followed by ...a5-a4 this is generally a good idea to gain some critical squares on the queenside. } 11. O-O Re8 12. Bd2 ( { An alternative was } 12. b3 Bf8 13. Rd1 g6 ( { maybe better is } 13. Bd2 Bg4 14. f3 Bd7 { followed by ...c7-c5 with counterplay } ) 
14. f3 Bg7 15. e4 dxe4 16. fxe4 c6 17. a4 $14 { when White achieves comfortable control of the centre, as in the game M. Gurevich – Polaczek, Philadelphia 1989. } ) 
 ( { PH: The issue with anytime White tries the plan of } 12. f3 { , Black has the reply } c5 $1 ) 
a4 13. Rae1 Bd7 { Placing the bishop on d7 is only a temporary necessity, and it can be followed by ...Qc8 and ...Bf5 (or ...Bh3) improving Black’s control of the light squares. } ( { PH: The computer likes } 13... c6 { here, solidifying the structure and asking White what he's going to do. Here again } 14. f3 { would be answered by } c5 $1 15. dxc5 Bxc5 { with good pressure on White's position. } ) 
14. Nf4 ( { The alternative } 14. f3 { is well met by } c5 $1 15. dxc5 Bxc5 $15 { when Black obtains a favourable version of the isolani due to White’s weakened central structure and the useful a4-pawn, which secures a strong outpost on c4 for one of Black’s knights. PH: Even } Bd6 { is fine, since after } 15. e4 { Black can strike with } c5 $1 16. e5 cxd4 { . } ) 
c6 15. e4 { Transposing into an isolated queen pawn structure. } ( { Unfortunately White’s position is not ideally suited for the more typical plan } 15. f3 { intending to follow with e3-e4, because of } Nc4 16. Bc1 ( { but not } 16. Nxa4 $2 Nxd2 $19 ) 
b5 { when Black manages to create some pressure on the centre and kingside, preventing White’s active plans. For example: } 17. Kh1 ( { or } 17. e4 $6 dxe4 18. fxe4 Qb6 19. Rd1 Bg4 $15 { when White’s centre is more of a weakness than a strength } ) 
Qb6 18. Rd1 b4 $1 { ∞ With good active play for Black. } ) 
dxe4 16. Nxe4 Nxe4 17. Bxe4 g6 { We have reached a critical position. There is already one minor piece off the board and White does not have clear attacking chances, therefore I find his position rather delicate, meaning that a few inaccuracies will lead to a much inferior endgame. } 18. h4 ( { An alternative was } 18. d5 { eliminating the isolated pawn: } cxd5 19. Nxd5 Nxd5 20. Bxd5 { However, Black can find complications with } Bb5 $5 ( { or } 20. h4 Bf6 $11 ) 
 { since White’s rook is trapped on f1: } 21. Qe4 $1 Ra6 ( { or } 21. h4 Bxf1 22. Qf3 Rf8 23. Kxf1 $11 { followed by Bh6 regaining the exchange } ) 
22. Bc3 Bxf1 23. Kxf1 Rf8 $13 { White seems to have sufficient compensation for the exchange. } ) 
Bd6 19. Kh2 { An inconsequential move. } ( { Logical was } 19. h5 Qf6 20. Qd3 { with level chances. } ) 
Qf6 20. Qd3 ( { Unfortunately } 20. d5 $2 { is no longer possible due to: } Nxd5 21. Bxd5 cxd5 22. Nxd5 $2 Qxh4 $19 ) 
Bg4 $15 { White’s position becomes unpleasant. } 21. Bg2 Bc7 22. Rxe8+ Rxe8 23. Rc1 Qf5 { Trading queens only emphasizes the weakness of the d4-pawn. } 24. Qxf5 Bxf5 25. Bf3 ( 25. d5 $5 { was probably better: } Rd8 $1 ( { incorrect is } 25. Bf3 Bxf4 $6 26. Bxf4 Nxd5 27. Bxd5 cxd5 28. Rc7 { when Black cannot maintain his advantage after } d4 29. Rxb7 Re2 30. Bh6 ) 
26. Bf3 ( { but not } 26. dxc6 $2 Rxd2 27. cxb7 Rc2 $1 { ∓ } ) 
Bxf4 27. Bxf4 Nxd5 28. Be5 Be6 $15 { White does not have enough counterplay for the pawn, but at least stays in the game with reasonable fighting chances. } ) 
h5 26. Be3 Be4 { It is a good decision to trade White’s light-squared bishop in order to gain control of the precious d5-square. } 27. Kg2 Bxf4 28. Bxf4 Bxf3+ 29. Kxf3 Nd5 $17 { It may be too soon for my assertion that Black has a clear advantage, but after analyzing the rest of the game the reader might start to agree with me. From a strategic point of view, matters are quite clear, as White has reached an endgame where the isolated d4-pawn is nothing but weak and blockaded. White’s bishop on f4 has no targets whatsoever and sees its action restricted by its own pawns. All Black needs is patience and some creativity to find the right plan, which Efimenko does quite well. } 30. Bd2 f6 31. Rc4 Ra8 ( { Of course not } 31... Nb6 $2 32. Rb4 $16 { winning a pawn. } ) 
32. Ke4 Kf7 33. Kd3 Ke6 34. f3 Ra6 $6 { This allows White an extra option. } 35. Bc1 $6 { Returning the favour. } ( { White had an opportunity to reduce his problems with } 35. g4 $5 Kd7 $15 { when things are less clear, but Black still preserves a small edge } ( { instead } 35. Bc1 Rb6 { is well met by } 36. Kc2 Rb3 37. gxh5 gxh5 38. Rxa4 Rxf3 39. Ra7 b5 40. Rh7 { when White has decent counterplay } ) 
 ) 
Kf5 { Preventing g3-g4 as well as preparing the key ...g6-g5-g4 advance. } 36. Ke2 g5 $1 { Choosing the right plan. By playing ...g5-g4 Black will manage to create a serious weakness on g3. } 37. Kf2 ( { Somewhat better was: } 37. hxg5 fxg5 38. Kf2 g4 { The only path to an advantage. } 39. Kg2 ( { but not } 39. fxg4 $2 hxg4 $17 { as Black will follow with ...Ke4 gaining territory and creating the serious threat of ...Kd3 } ) 
Rb6 ( { or } 39. Kf2 gxf3 40. Kxf3 Rb6 { transposing to } Rb6 ) 
40. Rxa4 Rb3 41. fxg4 Kxg4 42. Ra8 Rxg3 43. Kh2 ( { or } 43. Kf2 h4 $17 ) 
Nf6 $17 { White’s position is still very difficult due to the passed h-pawn and the poor placement of his king. } ) 
g4 38. fxg4+ $6 { This makes Black’s winning task much easier. } ( { An alternative was } 38. Kg2 gxf3 39. Kxf3 Rb6 40. Rxa4 Rb3 41. Kf2 Kg4 42. Ra8 Rf3 43. Ke2 Rxg3 $17 { when pushing the f-pawn should be enough to decide the game. } ) 
 ( { More stubborn was } 38. Bd2 Rb6 39. Bb4 $1 { (blocking the b-file to prevent the deadly penetration with ...Rb3) } Ke6 40. fxg4 hxg4 41. Ke2 f5 $17 { though Black has many plans to improve his position, such as ...Nf6-e4 or ...Kf7-g6-h5 followed by ...f5-f4 . } ) 
Kxg4 39. Rc2 { As often happens, passive defence is ineffective. } ( 39. Kg2 { is no better: } Rb6 40. Rxa4 Rb3 41. Ra8 Rxg3 42. Kf2 Kxh4 $19 ) 
Rb6 40. Re2 Rb3 $19 { The g3-pawn is lost, and the rest is simple. } 41. Re4+ Kh3 42. g4 hxg4 43. h5 f5 44. Re1 Kh2 45. h6 Rf3+ 46. Ke2 Rh3 47. Kf1 f4 48. Re2+ Kg3 49. Rg2+ Kf3 { Final remarks 1. Black’s queenside expansion with ...a7-a5-a4, gaining control of the light squares, played an essential role throughout the game and deserves imitation. 2. The reader might think Kuzubov did not know what he was doing, as he left his dark-squared bishop inside the pawn chain. This is not the case: the dark-squared bishop can be inside the pawn chain as long as White is planning the break e3-e4 which would render this bishop useful again. 3. White’s opening did not achieve more than equality. White lost the game because after e3-e4 was played, his inaccuracies forced him into an inferior endgame. A crucial mistake was the slow 19.Kh2 steering White into passive defence. } 
 0-1

